.TH YCA 1 "September 2026" "yca" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yca \- a 2-tier ECDSA certificate authority
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yca
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-store\fR \fIdir\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yca
operates a simple two-tier X.509 CA (offline-style root plus
signing CA): it initializes the hierarchy, issues and revokes end-entity
certificates, maintains signed CRLs, and exports artifacts. Storage is a
single SQLite database plus delivered PEM/DER files under the store
directory. Each CA key lives either software-encrypted in the store
(\fBinternal\fR backend) or on a PKCS#11 token/HSM (\fBpkcs11\fR backend),
declared by each CA's own \fBkey_backend\fR; with \fBpkcs11\fR a CA may
sit on its own token (\fBtoken_label\fR, defaulting to the shared
\fB[pkcs11] token_label\fR: one label everywhere means a single token,
a distinct label under \fB[root]\fR means split tokens, and a
\fBpkcs11\fR root over an \fBinternal\fR issuing key is the hybrid
layout). See the configuration file.
.PP
The configuration is organized in sections, which is also the granularity
at which it is locked: \fB[pki]\fR, an optional \fB[pkcs11]\fR,
\fB[root]\fR, and one \fB[ca.<purpose>]\fR per issuing CA. Each CA lists
the EE profiles it issues, and a profile belongs to exactly one CA: that
is how issuance picks an issuer. At
.B yca init
each section is snapshotted into the store database, which becomes the
source of truth; later edits to locked fields in the file are ignored with a
warning. Per\-issuance flexibility is \fB\-\-valid\fR, capped by
\fBee_valid_days\fR.
.PP
Subject DNs are encoded \fBC\fR, \fBO\fR, \fBCN\fR, from the most general
component to the most specific, as X.500 prescribes and as every publicly
trusted certificate does. The order is significant rather than cosmetic: a
\fBdirectoryName\fR name constraint is compared position by position. A CA
certificate always carries the full DN; \fB[ca.<purpose>] simple_dn =
true\fR reduces the leaves that CA issues to the bare \fBCN\fR, and is
refused on a CA carrying a profile whose subject must be organizational
(\fBemail\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Configuration file (default: \fByca.toml\fR in the current directory).
.TP
.BI \-\-store " dir"
Store/artifacts directory (default: \fB./store\fR). Created only by
\fBinit\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " path"
Log file (default: \fByca.log\fR next to the store directory). File
logging cannot be disabled: an empty \fIpath\fR is ignored with a warning,
an unwritable one aborts.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the release version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Initialize the PKI: create the root and every declared issuing CA (with
empty signed CRLs), in one root ceremony.
When any CA key uses the \fBinternal\fR backend, a store passphrase is
generated and shown once if \fBCA_STORE_PASSPHRASE\fR is unset. For every
key on a \fBpkcs11\fR backend, an existing token keypair must already be
labeled with the derived CA slug (\fB<slug_prefix>1\fR at init) to be
adopted (curve-checked); a missing one is generated on that key's token
under exactly that label. Init needs every configured token present.
Fails if the store is already initialized.
.TP
\fBcreate\fR \fIserver\fR|\fIclient\fR \fB\-\-cn\fR \fIcn\fR [\fB\-\-san\fR \fItype:name\fR ...] [\fB\-\-valid\fR \fIN\fR{s|m|h|d}]
Issue an end-entity certificate. \fB\-\-san\fR is repeatable with
\fItype\fR one of \fBdns\fR, \fBemail\fR, \fBip\fR, \fBuri\fR; servers
always get DNS:\fIcn\fR added, clients require at least one SAN. A
\fBuri\fR SAN must be an absolute, printable-ASCII URI, and one using
the \fBspiffe\fR scheme must be a valid SPIFFE ID; at most one
\fBuri\fR per certificate (an X509\-SVID carries exactly one).
\fB\-\-valid\fR
requests a shorter validity for this one issuance, range [5m,
\fBee_valid_days\fR]: the configured policy is both the default and the
ceiling, a request below it is always allowed; nothing is persisted. One
active certificate per (CN, profile); once the active one has
less than 33% of its lifetime left, an overlapping successor may be issued
(renewal window) and the old certificate simply expires.
.IP
Only the profiles whose key the CA may generate are offered here. The
\fIemail\fR profile is CSR\-only and is issued with \fBsign\fR: the CA
must never hold an S/MIME private key, which \fBcreate\fR would write
unencrypted under \fBee/\fR.
.TP
\fBenroll\fR \fB\-\-id\fR \fIid\fR
Enroll an identity (e.g. an email address) so it can request nonces and CSR
signatures. Fails if already enrolled.
.TP
\fBsign\fR \fIserver\fR|\fIclient\fR|\fIemail\fR \fB\-\-id\fR \fIid\fR \fB\-\-nonce\fR \fInonce\fR \fB\-\-csr\fR \fIcsr\fR [\fB\-\-valid\fR \fIN\fR{s|m|h|d}]
Issue an end-entity certificate from an external PKCS#10 CSR, gated by a
valid (\fIid\fR, \fInonce\fR) pair from \fBget nonce\fR; the nonce is
consumed on success (single use). \fIcsr\fR is inline PEM, \fB\-\fR for
standard input, or a file path (PEM/DER auto-detected). Only the public key
(ECDSA on the \fBee_curve\fR curve), the subject CN and the supported SANs
(dns, email, IPv4, URI) are taken from the CSR; every other requested
extension is ignored and the CA dictates the profile. The subject DN is
rebuilt from the CA's own configuration, so a requested
\fBorganizationName\fR or \fBcountryName\fR is replaced rather than kept.
The \fBuri\fR rules of \fBcreate\fR apply, so a CSR carrying more than
one URI SAN is refused.
\fB\-\-valid\fR behaves exactly as in \fBcreate\fR. Prints the CN on
standard output for piping into \fBget\fR \fB\-\-cn \-\fR; writes nothing
under \fBee/\fR (the private key stays with the requester).
.TP
\fBrevoke\fR \fIserver\fR|\fIclient\fR|\fIemail\fR|\fIca\fR [\fB\-\-cn\fR \fIcn\fR | \fB\-\-serial\fR \fIhex\fR] [\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIname\fR]
Revoke a certificate and rewrite the signed CRL. For a leaf profile the
entry goes on the CRL of the generation that issued the certificate, not
of whichever signs today. \fIca\fR revokes a signing CA
generation instead, selected by \fB\-\-cn\fR (its common name, or
\fB<purpose>-ca\fR for that CA's active generation) and never by
\fB\-\-serial\fR: the
entry goes on the \fBroot\fR CRL, the only thing that ever writes there,
and the updated CRL carries a fresh nextUpdate, so only publication is
left to do. Refused for the root, which relying parties drop from their
trust stores rather than revoke, and for the active generation of its own
purpose, which \fBrenew signing-ca\fR must replace first so that purpose
never has an issuance gap.
By \fB\-\-cn\fR the newest
active certificate for that name is revoked; \fB\-\-serial\fR (hex,
\fB:\fR separators and leading zeros tolerated) selects the exact
certificate (during a renewal overlap two can be active). \fIname\fR is a
case-insensitive CRLReason: \fBunspecified\fR (default),
\fBkeyCompromise\fR, \fBcACompromise\fR, \fBaffiliationChanged\fR,
\fBsuperseded\fR, \fBcessationOfOperation\fR, \fBcertificateHold\fR,
\fBremoveFromCRL\fR, \fBprivilegeWithdrawn\fR, \fBaACompromise\fR.
.TP
\fBadd\fR \fIsigning\-ca\fR \fB\-\-purpose\fR \fIp\fR
Create the issuing CA that \fB[ca.\fIp\fB]\fR declares but the store does
not hold: generation 1, signed by the active root, so this is a root key
ceremony. The section is locked into the store as it is created, which is
what lets a CA join an initialized store without re\-initializing it.
Refused when the file declares no such section, or when the store already
holds that purpose (rotate it with \fBrenew\fR instead). This is the one
command that reads \fByca.toml\fR rather than the store's snapshot, since
the section it creates is by definition not in the snapshot yet. Prints
the new CN on standard output.
.TP
\fBrenew\fR \fIsigning\-ca\fR [\fB\-\-purpose\fR \fIp\fR] \fB\-\-new\-cn\fR \fIcn\fR
Create the next generation of one issuing CA: a fresh key and that CA's
configured profile under the new display name \fIcn\fR, signed by
the active root, so this brings the root key online like a root CRL
refresh. The slug is derived (the CA's \fBslug_prefix\fR plus the
generation) and with \fBpkcs11\fR the keypair is generated on the token
under that label. The successor becomes the issuer of every new certificate; the
incumbent turns retiring and keeps publishing its own CRL until the last
certificate it signed has expired, so old chains keep validating. Every
other purpose is untouched. \fB\-\-purpose\fR is required once several
issuing CAs exist; with one it is inferred. Refused when \fIcn\fR already
names a generation, or when the new CA would outlive the root. Prints the
new CN on standard output.
.TP
\fBrefresh\fR \fIcrl\fR [\fBroot\fR|\fBsigning\fR|\fBall\fR]
Re-sign the published CRLs selected by the scope (default \fBall\fR) from
their current entry sets: same unexpired revocations, crlNumber+1, fresh
thisUpdate/nextUpdate. Entries whose certificate has expired are pruned
per RFC 5280 3.3 (an entry leaves the CRL only after one scheduled CRL
issued beyond the certificate's validity has carried it), so a CRL is
bounded by the revoked-and-unexpired set, not by the CA's age; the store
keeps every revocation. A scope covers every live generation of that CA:
after a rotation the retiring predecessor keeps publishing its own CRL
next to the active one's, and an expired generation is skipped. The signing CRL promises re-publication within 7
days, the root CRL within 183 days (both clamped to the issuing CA's
notAfter); the \fBsigning\fR scope never loads the root key. Run each
scope on a schedule shorter than its horizon (daily
\fByca-crl-refresh.timer\fR, quarterly \fByca-root-crl-refresh.timer\fR)
so relying parties never see a stale CRL. Trade-off of the long root
horizon: a relying party may keep serving a cached root CRL until its
nextUpdate, so after \fBrevoke\fR \fIca\fR publish the fresh one at once
instead of waiting for the quarterly timer.
.TP
\fBget\fR \fIserver\fR|\fIclient\fR|\fIemail\fR|\fIca\fR|\fIcrl\fR|\fIconfig\fR|\fInonce\fR [\fB\-\-cn\fR \fIcn\fR] [\fB\-\-id\fR \fIid\fR] [\fB\-\-encoding\fR \fBpem\fR|\fBder\fR] [\fB\-\-chain\fR]
Write a certificate, CRL, the effective (database) configuration, or an
enrollment nonce to standard output (default \fBpem\fR). For
\fIca\fR/\fIcrl\fR, \fB\-\-cn\fR takes \fBroot-ca\fR, \fB<purpose>-ca\fR, or
the CA common name; \fIconfig\fR takes no \fB\-\-cn\fR;
\fInonce\fR takes \fB\-\-id\fR instead and returns the pending nonce while
most of its 5-minute validity remains, rotating it otherwise. \fB\-\-cn\fR
\fB\-\fR reads the CN from standard input (piping from \fBsign\fR). Needs no
secret.
.IP
\fB\-\-chain\fR appends the issuers above the certificate, \fBpem\fR-encoded
and nearest first, stopping below the self\-signed root, which relying
parties already hold.
.TP
\fBlist\fR \fIfilter\fR [\fB\-\-tsv\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]
List certificates matching exactly one filter: \fB\-\-expiring\fR [\fIN\fR],
\fB\-\-expired\fR [\fIN\fR], \fB\-\-revoked\fR [\fIN\fR], \fB\-\-last\fR
[\fIN\fR] (window in days, 1\-398, default 30), or \fB\-\-cn\fR
\fIcn\fR|\fBroot-ca\fR|\fB<purpose>-ca\fR. Columns: CN, kind, serial
(truncated), expiry, status. \fB\-\-tsv\fR emits tab-separated output.
At most \fB\-\-limit\fR rows are printed (default 50, \fB0\fR for all); a
truncated listing says so on standard error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
An operation needs only the secrets of the CA keys it touches: issuance
and the \fBsigning\fR CRL scope the signing side's, \fBrevoke ca\fR and
the \fBroot\fR CRL scope the root side's, \fBinit\fR and
\fBrenew signing-ca\fR both.
.TP
.B CA_STORE_PASSPHRASE
Store passphrase (keys on the \fBinternal\fR backend). Generated and shown
once at init when unset.
.TP
.B CA_HSM_PIN
PKCS#11 user PIN of the signing token (\fBpkcs11\fR signing backend).
Exactly one login attempt per token per run: hardware tokens decrement a
PIN retry counter.
.TP
.B CA_HSM_ROOT_PIN
PKCS#11 user PIN of the root token (\fBpkcs11\fR root backend); falls
back to \fBCA_HSM_PIN\fR when unset (identical PINs, or the single-token
layout).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B yca.toml
Declarative configuration, in sections
(\fB[pki]\fR, \fB[pkcs11]\fR, \fB[root]\fR, \fB[ca.<purpose>]\fR); each
section is locked into the store when it is created.
.TP
.B <store>/ca-store.db
SQLite store: certificates, encrypted CA keys (internal backend),
revocations, configuration snapshot, query index.
.TP
.B <store>/ca/<slug>.{pem,crt,crl}
Published CA certificates (PEM and DER) and signed CRLs, named for the
AIA/CDP URLs.
.TP
.B <store>/ee/<cn-slug>.{crt,key}
Delivered end-entity credentials.
.TP
.B yca.log
Diagnostic log, next to the store directory (\fB\-\-log\fR overrides).
Errors are also mirrored to standard error.
.SH OPERATOR WRAPPER
In the distribution packages the
.B yca
on \fBPATH\fR is a shell wrapper; the CLI itself is
\fB/usr/libexec/yca/yca\fR (FreeBSD: \fB/usr/local/libexec/yca/yca\fR),
which the services and \fByca\-acme\fR call directly. The wrapper runs the
CLI as the \fByca\fR service account, through \fBsudo\fR unless invoked by
root, with umask 077 and the CA secrets from \fB/etc/yca/yca.env\fR
(FreeBSD: \fB/usr/local/etc/yca/yca.env\fR), read by root and never by the
caller's shell. On Linux it uses \fBsystemd\-run\fR(1) (a pseudo-terminal
when both standard input and output are terminals, pipes otherwise); on
FreeBSD \fBsu \-m\fR.
.PP
\fBCA_STORE_PASSPHRASE\fR, \fBCA_HSM_PIN\fR and \fBCA_HSM_ROOT_PIN\fR,
when exported by the caller (a ceremony PIN kept off disk), are passed on
by name, never on a command line; for a variable set in both places the
env file wins.
.PP
\fB\-\-config\fR and \fB\-\-store\fR default to \fB/etc/yca/yca.toml\fR and
\fB/var/lib/yca/store\fR (FreeBSD: \fB/usr/local/etc/yca/yca.toml\fR,
\fB/var/db/yca/store\fR) unless given. Invoked as \fByca\fR, or with
\fB\-\-help\fR/\fB\-\-version\fR as the first argument, it runs the CLI
unchanged. The CLI runs in the state directory as \fByca\fR, so a CSR in
the caller's files is passed on standard input (\fB\-\-csr \-\fR) or
inline.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error (details on stderr and in the log).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
yca init
yca create server \-\-cn server.example.ca \-\-san dns:example.ca
yca enroll \-\-id user@example.ca
yca sign server \-\-id user@example.ca \e
    \-\-nonce $(yca get nonce \-\-id user@example.ca) \e
    \-\-csr \- < server.csr | yca get server \-\-cn \-
yca revoke server \-\-cn server.example.ca \-\-reason keyCompromise
yca get crl \-\-cn signing\-ca > signing.crl
yca list \-\-expiring 7
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yca\-acme (1)
